WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.377
(host)
First impression
of the now-classic "Cujo"--

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.962
a mother's fight
to save her son's life

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:06.339
while being terrorized
by a rabid dog

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:07.590
blocking her escape.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.758
Second impression:

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.344
"Cujo" is a modern-day
hellhound,

00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:14.055
guarding the threshold between
the dead inside the house

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:16.766
and the mom clinging
to her son's life outside.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.519
Hellhounds are mostly
black-furred and startling,

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:20.770
with red, glowing eyes,

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:23.106
employed by a leader
of the afterlife.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.775
Take the terror dogs
from "Ghostbusters,"

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.361
servants of Gozer,
the all-powerful entity

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:29.821
from another dimension

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:33.283
who ruled over a dark,
underground realm of souls.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.118
Vinz Clortho, the Keymaster,

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.745
and Zuul, the Gatekeeper,

00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:40.582
take the form of horned,
red-eyed hellhounds on Earth.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:42.375
Tasked with guarding
the entrance

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:45.003
to a literal hell gate
to Gozer's realm,

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:48.590
they are an iconic anomaly
in modern popular culture

00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:51.718
for their unusual ability
to possess humans.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.345
Even hardcore dog lovers
would be hard-pressed

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:56.139
to call these canines "cuties."

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.808
The hounds of hell
may not try to sink their fangs

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.561
into your flesh
or tear you limb from limb--

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:03.813
every time--
but your eternal life

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.274
is entirely up for grabs.

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:09.319
So, are dogs
man's best friend

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.488
or underworld enforcers?

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:14.407
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:16.326
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.204
I'm using "hellhound" here
as an overarching term

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:21.664
to refer to preternatural
canine creatures

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:25.168
who are associated with
the afterlife or underworld.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.587
Many dogs in early folklore
and mythology

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.506
are connected to these places,

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:32.550
but that isn't to say that
they were all inherently evil.

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.219
In some ways,
early death dogs

00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:36.179
were guides and protectors.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:39.849
Take Anubis, the jackal
or jackal-headed Egyptian god

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.434
of the dead and mummification,

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:44.604
who protected the resting place
of the physical body

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:46.648
and guided the soul
to the afterlife.

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:48.733
Similarly,
Shyama and Sabala

00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:52.028
were Hindi hounds who guarded
the gods of death's gates,

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:55.532
each with two sets of eyes--
the better to protect you.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.952
Cerberus, the three-headed hound
who guards Hades,

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:00.995
keeps the souls
sequestered there

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:02.580
and keeps the heroes out.

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:04.249
He can be a good boy, though.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:06.292
I mean, he adorably
falls into a slumber

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.211
if you play him
a little music,

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.880
which you might want to do,
since otherwise,

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.131
he could just eat you.

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:14.509
In Mesoamerican
spiritual beliefs,

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:16.386
in some Latin American
traditions,

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.556
dogs guide the souls
of the dead to the next world.

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.683
And if you mistreated
a dog in life,

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:25.270
in death your soul was at risk
to wander for eternity.

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:27.814
In various Middle Eastern
and Indian traditions,

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:30.567
dogs were the escorts
into the afterlife,

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.735
brought into the room
of a dying person

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.404
to shepherd their passing.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.740
But these hounds aren't
inherently evil or scary.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.618
Why is it all hound
and no hell?

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.245
Funny you should ask.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.706
In Nordic sagas,
there's Garmr,

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:46.499
a bloodstained hellhound.

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:49.294
His mistress was
the death goddess Hel,

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.004
who presided
over the underworld,

00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:54.591
and his howl is said to signal
the beginning of Ragnarök.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.092
We also start to see the line

00:02:56.175 --> 00:03:00.013
between guardian and menace
blur as BCE turns to CE.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:03.600
Influential Roman poet Horace
wrote of a "dark companion,"

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:06.978
a "black dog" who symbolizes
misfortune and despair.

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:09.397
Greek philosopher
and historian Plutarch

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.150
went so far
as to call dogs "impure"

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:15.236
because of their association
with the goddess Hecate,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.114
who sacrificed dogs to serve
as guides for souls

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.490
in the afterlife.

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:21.951
The goddess herself
was even referred to

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.703
as a "black female dog."

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:27.332
Mosaics in Ancient Greece
show ferocious black dogs

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:29.125
guarding
the entrance to homes--

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:32.629
the earliest precursors
to "Beware of Dogs" signs.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.964
In the first
and second century CE,

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.674
dogs were incorporated
into folklore

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:38.635
as harbingers of misfortune,

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:41.387
which, given their close
relationship with humans

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.139
and the potential
to be aggressive,

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:44.599
made them easy targets.

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:46.476
With the rise
of the Medieval period,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:49.896
as we begin to separate
the animal from Homo sapiens,

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:52.398
dogs continue to be demonized,

00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:55.151
but also commonly prized
as loyal companions

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.569
and working animals.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:58.363
In Medieval
Middle Eastern culture,

00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:01.574
dogs were considered dirty,
disease-ridden creatures,

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:05.411
and all roots for the word "dog"
have negative connotations,

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:06.996
like "rabid" or "crazed."

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.541
Nevertheless, like the earlier
social and spiritual roles

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:12.168
of canines across the world,

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:15.296
they could also be guard dogs
or domestic helpers.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.383
The liminality of the dog
as inside/outside,

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:20.593
helper/harmer
in equal measure,

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:23.680
parallels the boundary
between life and death.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:27.475
Much like life and death,
we both fear and revere canines.

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:30.061
So, a supernatural version
of an everyday animal

00:04:30.144 --> 00:04:32.939
spanning that threshold
kind of makes sense.

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:35.483
Darkness is associated
with obscurity,

00:04:35.566 --> 00:04:37.694
representing
more negative connotations

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.028
than positive ones,

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:41.739
and why many of
these hellhounds were described

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:43.116
and depicted with black fur.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:45.368
In Medieval Europe,
black canines

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.786
appear in medical texts,

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:49.872
associated with melancholia
and other illnesses.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.709
In one 1800s CE French text,

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:55.253
it is written that one
could determine if a person

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:58.881
was terminally ill by taking
a tick from a black dog

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:00.800
and holding it
in the left hand.

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:03.344
If one presented
to a gravely ill person,

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:05.722
they turn toward it--
they're a goner.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.725
Black dogs continued to be
associated with illness

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.310
centuries later.

00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:12.228
Even in the late 18th century,

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.188
the black dog is linked
to depression.

00:05:14.272 --> 00:05:18.484
This terminology lingered into
the 19th and 20th centuries,

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:20.194
where bad moods
were attributed

00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:22.613
to having a "black dog"
on one's back

00:05:22.697 --> 00:05:24.240
or worrying you at home.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:26.701
Winston Churchill
would reportedly refer

00:05:26.784 --> 00:05:28.536
to his own struggles
with depression

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:29.912
as his "black dog."

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.081
Here on "Monstrum,"
we know that Christianity

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:35.084
loved to adopt and distort
older spiritual beliefs

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:36.335
and folkloric traditions,

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.296
especially
to support its agenda.

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:40.423
So it's not
all that surprising

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.924
the devil entered the equation,

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:44.427
taking the hellhounds
of folkloric traditions

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.929
and pre-Abrahamic
spiritual practices

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:49.223
into the devil
and witchcraft game.

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:52.727
Animals often serve
to normalize the supernatural

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.562
by marrying it
to something non-human

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:57.648
but still in the realm
of tangible familiarity.

00:05:57.732 --> 00:05:59.692
Connecting demonic possession
with an animal

00:05:59.776 --> 00:06:02.278
represented the beastly
or primal desire

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:03.780
of the human animal.

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:07.200
And, as we know, primal desires
are a no-no for the church.

00:06:07.283 --> 00:06:09.786
In the 1574
demonic possession case

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:12.080
of English lawyer
Robert Briggs,

00:06:12.163 --> 00:06:14.957
a demonic dog
plays a central figure.

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:16.876
Described
as horrifically ugly,

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:19.003
with a shaggy,
blackish-red coat,

00:06:19.087 --> 00:06:22.090
the dog would reportedly
follow Briggs around London.

00:06:22.173 --> 00:06:24.801
Briggs himself was terrified
of the monster,

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.512
and at one point,
even jumped into the Thames

00:06:27.595 --> 00:06:28.971
to shake the creature.

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.557
The dog was said to be
an earthly manifestation

00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:32.975
of the devil himself.

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:35.645
Other possession accounts
from the 16th century

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:38.564
include symptoms such as
sounding like a hellhound,

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:42.068
acting like a wild dog,
or communicating with dogs.

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:43.986
In a case
at the turn of the century,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.823
the devil communed with
his victim, William Sommers,

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:49.242
in the form
of a talking black dog.

00:06:49.325 --> 00:06:51.869
Hellhounds seem to be
primarily associated

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:53.454
with transient spaces,

00:06:53.538 --> 00:06:56.624
whether those are spaces
of physical travel and movement,

00:06:56.707 --> 00:06:58.167
like roadways and gates,

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:00.419
or places of
metaphysical transformation--

00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:03.422
graveyards or execution sites,
for instance.

00:07:03.506 --> 00:07:06.259
English folk culture
is perhaps where this trend

00:07:06.342 --> 00:07:07.885
is most obvious,

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:10.096
but it's by no means exclusive.

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:13.182
Great Britain, Ireland,
France, Scandinavia, Germany--

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:16.644
all of these place tell tales
of the black dog--

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:19.355
a special variation
of the hellhound trope

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.107
distinguished
by its massive red eyes

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.192
and long, thick,
black coat

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:26.320
whose presence always foretells
death and disaster

00:07:26.404 --> 00:07:27.697
for those who see it.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.657
Wandering spirits
and lost souls

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.535
are often said to take
the form of spectral dogs.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:36.372
In German lore, they appear
as shaggy dogs with black fur

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:38.541
and unnaturally glowing eyes.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.334
Although,
in a unique quirk,

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:42.628
if any specific breed is noted
in the description

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:45.548
of these German creatures,
it's often the poodle.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.426
Seems a bit odd,
unless you know that the poodle

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:50.887
traces its heritage
back to Germany

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.889
where they were bred
as duck-hunters.

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:56.267
Ghost dogs with black coats
and red eyes are reported

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.727
all across England,

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.313
sometime under the name
"black shuck"--

00:08:00.396 --> 00:08:03.191
a regional variation
of the black-dog motif

00:08:03.274 --> 00:08:06.277
specific to the eastern
countryside of England.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.529
The monster is said to be
a demonic being,

00:08:08.613 --> 00:08:10.072
either a hound from hell,

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:12.158
a demon familiar,
or both.

00:08:12.241 --> 00:08:14.577
It may be accompanied
by a foul smell.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.245
Shuck comes from "Shucky,"

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.748
a local term
for hairy or shaggy,

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:21.334
or the Old English word
for "demon."

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:23.085
Some scholars connect
the black shuck

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:25.129
and other demonic dogs
in England

00:08:25.213 --> 00:08:27.256
to the legend
of the wild hunt--

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.259
a spectral hunting party that
seeks the souls of the living,

00:08:30.343 --> 00:08:33.888
the first written account
of which appears in 1127.

00:08:33.971 --> 00:08:37.308
The first written recording
of the black shuck, however,

00:08:37.391 --> 00:08:39.602
appears in 1577.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:41.270
According to this early legend,

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:44.440
during mass at an Anglican
church in Blythburgh,

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:46.108
a clap of thunder rang out

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:48.611
before the doors to the church
slammed open,

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:52.156
revealing a giant black dog
who tore through the building,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.910
killing two people before
causing the steeple to collapse.

00:08:55.993 --> 00:08:58.287
That same day,
another church in Bungay

00:08:58.371 --> 00:09:01.123
experienced a black shuck
visit as well.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.751
The same dog
stormed into the building

00:09:03.834 --> 00:09:05.336
accompanied by lightning,

00:09:05.419 --> 00:09:08.839
clamping onto the necks of
two congregants, killing them.

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:11.801
The floors of both churches
bore scorch marks

00:09:11.884 --> 00:09:13.886
where the dog's paws
had touched--

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.973
ones that some swear
can still be seen today.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:20.476
Unsurprisingly, the dog
was associated with the Devil.

00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:22.478
The prevalence of black dogs
in British folklore,

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:26.357
literature, and oral tradition
from the 17th century onward

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.694
is responsible for dominating
popular culture's understanding

00:09:29.777 --> 00:09:31.195
of spectral hounds.

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:33.281
Take the 18th century case
of the old woman

00:09:33.364 --> 00:09:35.908
and the chimneysweep
from Hertfordshire, England.

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:38.577
As the story goes,
an old woman was drowned

00:09:38.661 --> 00:09:40.788
for her suspected use
of witchcraft--

00:09:40.871 --> 00:09:43.916
a murderous deed carried out
by a chimneysweeper

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.586
who himself was publicly hanged
near the spot.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.005
After this second death,

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:50.923
a black dog appeared
in the area.

00:09:51.007 --> 00:09:53.843
As big as a Newfoundland
but malnourished,

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:56.429
the shaggy, black-haired
creature's long teeth

00:09:56.512 --> 00:09:58.264
protruded
from his grinning mouth,

00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:01.017
his eyes glowing
like balls of fire.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.685
Despite
his frightening appearance,

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:05.521
the creature never did more
than suddenly appear,

00:10:05.604 --> 00:10:07.815
giving unsuspecting travelers
a fright.

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:10.568
In Lincolnshire,
a taller and thinner version

00:10:10.651 --> 00:10:12.945
of the black dog appears
near a deep pond

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:14.655
surrounded by trees.

00:10:14.739 --> 00:10:17.700
Water has long been associated
with liminality.

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:19.994
Ponds, rivers, wells,
and even puddles

00:10:20.077 --> 00:10:22.038
could be portals
to the otherworld,

00:10:22.121 --> 00:10:24.707
and the dangerous nature
of all bodies of water

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.083
links them to death.

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:28.210
Some rationalize sightings
of these monsters

00:10:28.294 --> 00:10:31.881
as culturally based fear
responses to stressful events--

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:34.133
eerie nighttime encounters,
death,

00:10:34.216 --> 00:10:36.010
or destructive thunderstorms.

00:10:36.093 --> 00:10:38.346
Others say the sightings
can be attributed

00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:39.764
to panthers or large cats

00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:42.725
that escape from
private residences and zoos.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:46.145
Some insist the sulfur smell
that may accompany the black dog

00:10:46.228 --> 00:10:48.439
is not the sign
of a hellish origin,

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:50.483
but the result
of hydrogen sulfide gas

00:10:50.566 --> 00:10:53.361
produced by the rotting
vegetation of the wetlands,

00:10:53.444 --> 00:10:55.780
where many of the sightings
took place.

00:10:55.863 --> 00:10:57.990
The boom of published
folklore collections

00:10:58.074 --> 00:10:59.367
in the 19th century

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:01.952
also meant hellhounds
began to pop up again

00:11:02.036 --> 00:11:03.579
in fictional literature.

00:11:03.662 --> 00:11:05.831
Famous Victorian texts
like "Jane Eyre,"

00:11:05.915 --> 00:11:08.334
"The Hound of Baskervilles,"
and "Dracula"

00:11:08.417 --> 00:11:10.544
solidified the presence
of monster dogs

00:11:10.628 --> 00:11:13.255
in 19th century
Gothic literature.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.007
In more modern
literature and film,

00:11:15.091 --> 00:11:18.636
the idea of hellhounds
and spectral dogs of all sorts

00:11:18.719 --> 00:11:20.179
has become more varied.

00:11:20.262 --> 00:11:22.848
They've played tribute
to ancient Greek mythology

00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:25.601
and continue to serve
as metaphors of ill omen,

00:11:25.684 --> 00:11:28.604
but they've also appeared
as happy ghost hounds

00:11:28.687 --> 00:11:30.815
and lovable spirit guides.

00:11:30.898 --> 00:11:33.359
These varied
and various representations

00:11:33.442 --> 00:11:36.112
display our changing view
of canines

00:11:36.195 --> 00:11:38.364
and, arguably,
our renewed appreciation

00:11:38.447 --> 00:11:39.907
for older traditions.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.411
So, despite modern society's
infatuation with puppers,

00:11:43.494 --> 00:11:46.122
doggos, and booping
the snout of fluffers,

00:11:46.205 --> 00:11:48.707
we can't seem
to fully remove the dog

00:11:48.791 --> 00:11:49.959
from the hellhound.

00:11:50.042 --> 00:11:51.460
Nrhh.
Look it up.

00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:52.795
Hertfordshire, England!

00:11:52.878 --> 00:11:54.839
I don't think there's
any reason to add the "D,"

00:11:54.922 --> 00:11:55.840
I'm just doing it.

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:57.466
It's probably "Briggs."

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.261
Briggs.
Hertfordshire. Perfect.

